    "---\n",
    "# Part A · Read before you run\n",
    "\n",
    "## A1. Why this session matters\n",
    "\n",
    "### The problem, in one story\n",
    "Imagine a bank whose assistant reads every customer complaint and sends it to one queue: **billing**, **technical**, or **general**. It works in the demo. Then it goes live, and three things happen in the first quarter.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **The provider has to change.** The risk team rules that complaints with account details must go to a model the bank hosts itself (the data-residency rule from Session 10). But the vendor's address and model name are written into fourteen files. A configuration change becomes three weeks of edits and re-testing.\n",
    "2. **The same complaint gets two answers.** *\"I upgraded my plan but I'm still charged the old price and the new features don't show.\"* On Monday it goes to *billing*; sent again on Tuesday, word for word, it goes to *technical*. The code did not change. The call used a high **temperature**, so the model sometimes picked a less likely answer. An auditor asks the team to reproduce Monday's decision, and they cannot.\n",
    "3. **Replies stop mid-sentence.** A very small **max_tokens** was set to save money. Explanations are cut off, and with a reasoning model some replies are empty: the hidden thinking used the whole budget (Session 10).\n",
    "\n",
    "None of these is a problem with the model. **They are problems with how the call was made.** That is today's topic.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What you learn today, and what each one prevents\n",
    "\n",
    "| You learn to... | It prevents... | The everyday name |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Call any provider through **one function** (`ask()`), changing only `BASE_URL`, `API_KEY`, `MODEL` | Weeks of rewrites when a provider, price or policy changes | Swap the plug, not the appliance |\n",
    "| Use the **system message** on purpose | A model that falls back to a chatty default personality | The job description |\n",
    "| Set **temperature** low for decisions | The same ticket landing in different queues on different days | The randomness dial |\n",
    "| Set **max_tokens** and **stop** deliberately | Cut-off or empty replies, and answers that ramble past the shape you need | The ceiling and the tripwire |\n",
    "| **Verify** a model name live instead of trusting a written one | Code that breaks when a vendor retires a model name | Check, don't memorise |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Where this is used in industry\n",
    "- **Banking and insurance:** firms often use more than one provider, for example a hosted API for general text and a self-hosted model for data that must stay in-house. Provider-in-configuration makes that possible.\n",
    "- **LLM gateways:** one routing layer in front of several providers (open-source libraries such as LiteLLM, or a cloud gateway) lets teams switch or fail over by configuration. Session 21 builds a small version with LangChain.\n",
    "- **One request shape everywhere:** Groq, Google's Gemini endpoint, Ollama and self-hosting servers such as vLLM accept the OpenAI-compatible shape. Learn it once.\n",
    "- **Low temperature for decisions:** complaint routing in banks, claims triage in insurance, ticket triage in telecom and retail; clinical-note summaries in healthcare, where the same note should give the same summary. Creative drafting uses higher values.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why interviewers care\n",
    "These questions come up in AI-engineer and GenAI-developer interviews:\n",
    "- *\"How would you design an LLM application so you can switch providers without rewriting code?\"*\n",
    "- *\"What does temperature do, and what would you set it to for a classifier?\"*\n",
    "- *\"What is the difference between temperature and top_p?\"*\n",
    "- *\"Is temperature 0 fully deterministic?\"*\n",
    "- *\"What happens if max_tokens is too low, especially on a reasoning model?\"*\n",
    "\n",
    "By the end of today you can answer each one with a definition, an example from your own lab, and one honest limit. Part C has model answers.\n",
    "\n",
    "<details><summary><b>▸ Read more: why does a \"standard\" request shape exist at all?</b></summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "OpenAI's chat-completions API was one of the first widely used LLM APIs, so many tools were written for its shape (`model`, `messages`, sampling settings; a reply with `choices` and `usage`). Other vendors accept that shape so developers need not learn a new one. \"Compatible\" covers the common features; vendor-specific ones may differ, so teams still test each provider.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
