    "## A3. The words you need today\n",
    "\n",
    "Read these once now. Each term appears in bold the first time it is used in Part B.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**1. Provider**\n",
    "- **Definition:** the company or program that runs a model and answers your requests. Today: Groq, Google (Gemini), and Ollama on your laptop.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** a place. A provider is *where* the brain lives.\n",
    "- **Example:** Groq at `https://api.groq.com/openai/v1`.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** the model. One provider offers many models, and one open-weight model can be offered by many providers.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S12, S21, S58–S60.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**2. Model**\n",
    "- **Definition:** the trained network that writes the answer, chosen by its name in the request.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** the brain that answers.\n",
    "- **Example:** `openai/gpt-oss-20b` on Groq (the course default), `llama3.2:3b` on Ollama.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S10, S12, S15.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**3. The three settings: `BASE_URL`, `API_KEY`, `MODEL`**\n",
    "- **Definition:** where to send the request (the address), who you are (a secret key), and which model should answer. They live in `.env`, never in code.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** the address on the envelope, your PIN, and the name of the person you want to reach.\n",
    "- **Example:** for Ollama: `http://localhost:11434/v1`, the word `ollama` (no real key needed), `llama3.2:3b`.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** settings you may share. Never paste a key in chat, code or a screenshot; if it leaks, revoke it and create a new one.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S12, S22, S55, S60.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**4. OpenAI-compatible endpoint**\n",
    "- **Definition:** an address that accepts the same request shape as OpenAI's chat-completions API and answers in the same shape, though another vendor runs it.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** same language, different speaker.\n",
    "- **Example:** Google's `https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/` lets the `openai` Python library talk to Gemini.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** \"uses OpenAI's models\". Only the *format* is shared.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S12, S21.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**5. Client**\n",
    "- **Definition:** the Python object that holds one address and one key and sends requests for you: `OpenAI(base_url=..., api_key=...)`.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** a phone with one number saved.\n",
    "- **Example:** `groq_client()`, `gemini_client()`, `ollama_client()`: three phones of the same make.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** a connection. Creating a client contacts nobody; the first real request does.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S12, S20.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**6. Provider-agnostic code**\n",
    "- **Definition:** code that works unchanged with any provider, because only the three settings change.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** swap the plug, not the appliance.\n",
    "- **Example:** `ask()` is the same for Groq, Gemini and Ollama.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** \"every provider gives the same answer\". The *code* is the same; answers, speed and price differ.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S21, S58–S60.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**7. Messages list and roles**\n",
    "- **Definition:** the conversation you send: a list of dictionaries, each with a `role` and `content`. **system** sets behaviour, **user** is the person, **assistant** is the model's earlier replies.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** the job description (system), the customer at the counter (user), the clerk's notes of what they already said (assistant).\n",
    "- **Example:** `[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are terse.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is an EMI?\"}]`\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** a single prompt string. The system message stays the same on every request; the user message changes.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** every session; S14, S19 (chat memory), S20 (tool results), S34 onwards.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**8. Token usage**\n",
    "- **Definition:** the counts returned with each reply: **prompt tokens** (what you sent), **completion tokens** (what it wrote), and the **total**. Price and rate limits are counted in tokens.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** the itemised bill.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** words. Each provider has its own tokenizer (Session 9), so the same text can count differently.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S15 (token meter), S19 (token budgets).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**9. temperature**\n",
    "- **Definition:** a number (0 to roughly 2) that sets how much randomness goes into picking each next token. 0 is as close to \"always pick the most likely token\" as the provider allows.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** the randomness dial.\n",
    "- **Example:** classify a ticket at `0.0`; brainstorm slogans at `0.9`.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** creativity. Higher temperature means more varied choices, not better ideas, and sometimes less coherent ones.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S15 (self-consistency), S34 (agents keep it low).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**10. top_p**\n",
    "- **Definition:** another way to shape the same randomness: choose only among the most likely tokens whose probabilities add up to `top_p`.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** a shortlist.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** something to set together with temperature. Teams usually change one and leave the other at its default; today's `ask()` does not expose it.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**11. max_tokens**\n",
    "- **Definition:** a hard ceiling on the reply's length in tokens. The model is stopped when it reaches it, even mid-sentence.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** the page limit, not the target length.\n",
    "- **Example:** `max_tokens=15` cuts an explanation after a few words.\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** \"the length I want\". On a reasoning model the hidden thinking counts too, so a low limit can leave nothing visible.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S10, S15, S19.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**12. Stop sequence (`stop`)**\n",
    "- **Definition:** a string that ends generation the moment it appears. The string itself is not included.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** a tripwire for the output.\n",
    "- **Example:** `stop=[\"\\n\"]` keeps only the first line.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S13 and the structured-output labs.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**13. Drift**\n",
    "- **Definition:** the same input getting different outputs on repeated runs with the same settings.\n",
    "- **Analogy:** a coin flip hidden inside a decision.\n",
    "- **Example:** `temperature=1.0  DRIFTED  ['billing', 'technical', 'billing']`\n",
    "- **Often confused with:** a bug in your code. It is how sampling works; temperature controls it.\n",
    "- **Comes back in:** S15 (voting over samples), S17 (evaluation), S52."
   ]
  },
