    "### What and why\n",
    "A model picks **one token at a time** (Session 9). At each step it *samples* one token from a list of possible next tokens, and four *sampling* settings shape that pick.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **temperature** (0 to roughly 2) controls how much randomness goes into picking the next token. 0 is as close to deterministic as a provider allows; higher values let the model pick less-likely tokens more often.\n",
    "- **top_p** is an alternative way to control the same thing — restrict the choice to the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to `top_p`. Most labs here use temperature alone; the two are rarely combined.\n",
    "- **max_tokens** caps the length of the reply. Set it too low on a reasoning model and you can get an empty answer, as you saw in Session 10.\n",
    "- **stop** ends generation the moment a given string appears — useful for forcing a short, structured answer.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The idea: what temperature does to the choice\n",
    "At each step every possible next token gets a score; temperature reshapes the scores before the pick. The numbers below are **made up for illustration**, but the effect is real:\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    " chance of each label at one step         temperature 0.3      temperature 0.7      temperature 1.0\n",
    " ───────────────────────────────          ───────────────      ───────────────      ───────────────\n",
    " billing                                  ████████████  79%    █████████    59%     ████████     53%\n",
    " technical                                ███           21%    █████        34%     █████        35%\n",
    " general                                  ▏              1%    █             7%     ██           12%\n",
    "                                          ▲ sharp: nearly always billing           ▲ flat: often not\n",
    "```\n",
    "At temperature 0 the top choice is (nearly) always taken; as temperature rises, the chances flatten. **top_p** works differently: with `top_p=0.8` and the 1.0 column, only billing and technical stay on the shortlist (53% + 35% ≥ 80%); general is never picked.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Analogy.** Temperature is how loosely a manager follows their first instinct; top_p is a shortlist; max_tokens is a page limit that stops the writer mid-sentence; stop is a tripwire that takes the pen away. `ask()` exposes temperature, max_tokens and stop, but **not** top_p, on purpose: one dial at a time.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What the next cell does (max_tokens)\n",
