    "## C3. Interview practice\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. \"How would you design an LLM application so you can switch providers without rewriting code?\"**\n",
    "*Model answer:* Keep the provider in configuration (base URL, key, model name from the environment) and route every call through one function. Many providers accept the OpenAI-compatible shape, so one client library covers them. *In this lab:* `ask()` ran unchanged against Groq, Gemini and Ollama; switching the course default took three lines in `.env` and a kernel restart. *Honest limit:* compatibility covers common features only; model names, limits and quality still differ, so each provider must be tested.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. \"What does temperature do, and what would you set it to for a classifier?\"**\n",
    "*Model answer:* Temperature reshapes the probabilities of the next token: low values make the top choice dominant, high values flatten them. For a classifier, use 0 or close to it. *In this lab:* the borderline sweep at 0, 0.3, 0.7 and 1.0 showed, through `stable` versus `DRIFTED`, where labels began to change. *Honest limit:* low temperature makes answers repeatable, not correct; and three runs per temperature is a small sample.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. \"What is the difference between temperature and top_p?\"**\n",
    "*Model answer:* Temperature changes how spread out the probabilities are; top_p keeps only the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p. Teams usually adjust one and leave the other at its default. *In this lab:* `ask()` exposes only temperature; section 4's illustration showed a 12% option dropped by `top_p=0.8`. *Honest limit:* exact behaviour and allowed ranges vary by provider; check the provider's documentation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. \"Is temperature 0 fully deterministic?\"**\n",
    "*Model answer:* No. It is as close to \"always pick the most likely token\" as the provider allows, but hosted services can still vary slightly, for example because of how requests are batched and computed. *In this lab:* temperature 0 was usually stable across three runs; if anyone saw a change at 0, that is the reason. *Honest limit:* if you need exact reproducibility (for audit), store the inputs, the settings and the output of each decision, rather than relying on re-running the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "**5. \"What happens if max_tokens is too low, especially on a reasoning model?\"**\n",
    "*Model answer:* The reply is cut at the ceiling, often mid-sentence. A reasoning model's hidden thinking counts against the same budget, so a low limit can leave nothing visible. *In this lab:* `max_tokens=15` versus `150`; and an earlier `classify_ticket` with `max_tokens=10` returned empty replies (`INVALID:''`) on the course's reasoning model, which is why it now uses 1000. *Honest limit:* the right value depends on model and task; use it as a safety ceiling and ask for brevity in the prompt."
   ]
